e other hand when they are exposed  to  certain patterning factors stem cells  have the ability of differentiating into  many different specialized  cell types this last characteristic  can be better explained by the concept  of cellular potency  cell potency in fact describes the  ability of the cells  to differentiate into other cell types  and the more cell type a cell can  differentiate  into the greater is its potency  in this webinar we will focus on  solution for pluripotent stem cells  pscs that are known to be able to  differentiate into all the embryonic  derivatives  there are mainly two types of pscs  that can be distinguished according to  their  origin so we have embryonic stem cells  that are originally derived from the  inner cell mass of a developing embryo  whereas differently induced pluripotent  stem cells  ipscs are derived from already  differentiated somatic cells  by cellular reprogramming other than  this difference in in the origin  embryonic stem cells  and induced pluripotent stem cells are  actually extremely  uh similar in terms of cultivation  morphology marker expression  and also experimental handling  also in regard of the differentiation  potential they show similar properties  and they can both give rise to cells  that derive  from the three embryonic lineages so  ectoderm mesoderm and endoderm  while working with pscs there are three  main  main steps that come into play  if we aim to generate new ipsc  lines directly from donors then  the first main task is the reprogramming  independently if we work with embryonic  or  induced reporters themselves they both  need to be  cultivated for further expansion  and finally the key step in psc  research is the differentiation process  that  allows us to have a functional and  appropriate cellular model  that we can use than for our experiments  so as you may anticipate each one of  these main steps  will require further subs tasks so let's  start  by having a better look to pse culture  before going into detail let's see how  pscs  are usually cultivated so classically  pacs have been cultured in presence  of supportive feeder cells  such as for example mitotically  inactivated mouse embryonic fibroblast  that supports the growth of pscs through  the secretion  of various extracellular molecules  unfortunately with this type of culture  is not  actually really possible to quantify  and qualify the factors that are  secreted by the feeders  and therefore it's really difficult to  provide a  standardized culturing condition in  every experiment so this is the reason  why  feeder-free cultivation system has been  developed  and are now actually the predominant  cultivation system in psc research  so this type of culturing basically  replaced the  need of feeder by providing on one side  a culturing matrix where the cells can  adhere  and and lay and on the other side  by providing an optimized cultural media  that provides the cells with all the  nutrients and the factors that  would have been secreted by by the  feeder cells  so in the light of it is easy to  understand  how a feeder-free cultivation system  should  always spread affair  but what to do in case  you are still using a feeder culture  system  well there are actually uh several  methods that can be used to switch from  a feeder to a seeder-free condition  in fact as you may see here on the left  the removal of the feeder cells can be  done for example  by selective adhesion that takes  advantage  of the preferential adherence of feeders  to uncoated culture plates or  it can be done by winning of the feeder  cells  over several passages or finally it can  be done by  magnetic separation so the first two  approaches are time consuming and do not  always manage to remove all feeder cells  whereas in contrast the magnetic  isolation that targets  feeder specific markers is way  faster and simple for example  here on the right you may see the flow  analysis of  pse cultures before and after