e the additional advantage of  allowing  a more flexible feeding schedule  so for example here you may see some  different  feeding strategies that can be adopted  when using stem max ips brew  so we can have a classical everyday  feeding  or we can have every other day  feeding or finally we can have a  so-called weekend-free feeding so  no feeding for to consecutive days  after some days in culture  psc will reach confluence and they will  need to be splitted  in additional culture plates and as you  might  already know splitting the psc culture  is a delicate  time point and of course specific  techniques  and reagents are necessary so that we  can maintain  a stable line in general  it's important to keep in mind that  different  lines have also different growth  kinetics  and therefore the splitting should  always be  assessed by having a look at the cells  under a microscope  generally uh we can say that it's a good  practice to keep  culture in a in a subconfluent stage  where we can still see the colonies  being separated among each other clearly  classical splitting techniques so for  example by  trypsin edition do not work  very well with pscs because the  detachment must be  really gentle so that we can maintain  maximum viability  and we can avoid spontaneous  differentiation  there are different methods to passage  psc culture  so the cotton paste method consists in  dividing the  psc colony with a needle under a  microscope  and actually this method takes  kind of a long time and it can be used  just if you are working with a low  number of culture  therefore we generally do not recommend  it  the other two methods are actually more  suited when  working with more than one line at the  time  and they are generally weaker  so for example splitting the culture by  single cell passaging  so by creating a cell suspension  is advisable when we need to perform a  cell counting before sitting  or we need to analyze the cells  these are the cases because this is a  relatively more hard method and  therefore  is not really well suited for routine  passaging  for routine passaging we advise in fact  to use  reagents that allow for the so-called  cluster passaging with this method  uh pscs are detached from the culture  metrics  as small aggregates and those this  allows  the preservation of cell to cell  contacts that are  pivotal in the maintenance of a  pluripotent phenotype  how does it work so here you can see a  time course detachment of psc colonies  done using the stamax passaging solution  as you can see after adding the solution  the cells will start to progressively  lift from the matrix  after about five minutes clusters can be  completely detached from the metrics by  just by pipetting up and down and  if needed the aggregates can be reduced  in smaller pieces by keeping on  pipetting up and down finally  the cell cluster can be transferred  and seeded into new cultureware  so during culture  batches of cells should always be frozen  in order to have always some cellular  backups  cryopreservation is not only important  as a good laboratory practice  but also for the creation of large  repositories  and by banks here you can see some  advices  so always freeze back up batches of  cells at an  early passage of course you should  always freeze just good quality cells  and finally the more defined the  freezing solution  is the more consistent your towing  efficiency will be  when using a defined freezing solution  in fact  we can expect consistent performances of  the psc  culture after towing  here you may see an assessment of cell  recovery  and viability of psc lines that were  originally frozen in stem max cryoboo  freezing solution and thought in stem  max ips broom  after throwing you can see that the  cells  show a high and reproducible recovery  rate  as well as viability and they do not  have a drastic alteration  in the doubling time additionally  you can see here below that the psc  lines  retain all their typical characteristics  so we quickly mentioned that psc  must show some specific characteristic  let's now look in more in detail what  these  are and what we mean by the term  psc characterization  so pse characterization includes  all the assays that are usually  performed  in order to confirm the pluripotency the  quality  the identity and the safety of a  pluripotent cell  line and this is a critical point  and the comprehensive characterization  is  mandatory when establishing new lines  and also as a good practice  during propagation of already  established line  so during psc characterization we  actually want to assess  first if the line has a typical  morphology  then if the expression of markers  is consistent with the pluripotent state  third if the line can differentiate into  cells that's derived from the three  embryonic lineages  and finally if the line has a normal  karyotype  when the line passes all these tests  then  it can be considered to be a high  quality psc line  so let's start with the first and  actually the  easiest feature to assess so the  cell and colony morphology being  a pst extremely sensitive cells  one of the first events that happens  when a line  loses its its characteristic is the  change in morphology  an optimal psc line should in fact  grow as a homogeneous and monolayer  culture  whereas the cell itself presents a scar  cytoplasm and it grows tightly packed  with other cells  forming colonies that are characterized  by having  sharp and defined borders here on the  right  you may see an example of pse morphology  and we strongly advise to monitor  the cellular morphology as often  as possible  in fact if the cells are  cultured under suboptimal condition  or they are exposed to environmental  stress  or if the line is just not a good  quality one  the psc may go an unwanted  spontaneous differentiation and here you  can see  the most common signs of alteration in  psc morphology  so starting from the left you may see an  example of  flat outgrowing of big cells  then an example of a colony that has  not really well-defined borders and  finally an example of central  differentiation  and loss of of the monolayer growth  so what should we do uh if we see  uh one of these cases in our culture  well the best solution would be of  course to  throw a batch of the same line that was  frozen  at the earlier passage unfortunately  we know all that this is not always  possible  and therefore an alternative approach  can be  to perform a positive selection of  pscs by pancreatic isolation  in fact the plcs that are still  expressing stem cell specific markers  can be isolated with a magnetic  separation  so the cells are harvested and  we are going to generate a single cell  suspension  then the cells are magnetically labeled  and in this case the labeling is for a  specific pac marker  trau 160 and allows us to target  just the cells that we want to to  maintain  these cells are going to be retained in  the magnetic fields  and after that they can be  eluted from the column then plated and  then we can start our culture again  as you can see from the plot  on the upper part of this slide  in this way we can efficiently and  quickly remove  differentiated cells so that we can  generate a pure  and homogeneous population of pscs for  subsequent culture or for  differentiation  second characteristic that psc needs to  show  is a stable and high expression of  intracellular and surface markers  that are well known to be associated  with a pluripotent phenotype  the expression can be monitored either  by  immunofluorescence or by flow cytometry  we generally advise for flows and flow  cytometry  uh since this allows not only  a qualitative assessment but also  quantitative evaluation of your  culture here you may see an example of  the assessment of pluripotency marker  expression  in one ipfc line  with our multi-color panel for flow  cytometry  so the antibody panel described here  enables the detection of both surface  and intracellular markers the percentage  of  of the marker expression is then plotted  in the matrix below and can help us  to quickly assess the phenotype of the  line  so as you can see the line on the left  shows high expression  marking red of pluripotency markers  such as ca4 160 sox2 octrim  whereas it shows a low expression of a  differentiation marker  in green and marker is cd15  conversely on the right panel we can see  the expression profile of this  of differentiated cell line  let's now move to the assessment of  pluripotency  so pfc by definition need to be  able to differentiate into cellular  types that derive from the three  embryonic germ layers so another  mesoderm and ectoderm  this property can be assessed in various  way  both in vitro and in vivo  classically pluripotency has been tested  in vivo  using the teratoma assay teratomas  are a distinct type of tumors that are  made up  from several different types of tissue  uh in the assay putative pluripotent  stem cells are  implanted into a immune compromised  mouse  where they will proliferate and  differentiate to form a teratoma  just in the case they are pluripotent  nevertheless this method is of course  long  and certainly requires ethical approval  and it's not really well suited for  assessment  in a routine way of your of your  cultures  in these cases the in vitro approach  is to be preferred we have two  assays that are currently being used  one is the embryo body formation assay  and the other one is the trillionaire  differentiation assay  to the embryo body assay  is based on the fact that high quality  pscs  when grown as a three-dimensional  aggregate  in the absence of a patterning factor  will spontaneously differentiate in  cellular types that derive from the  three embryonic germ layers  these spherical aggregates actually  slightly resemble  an embryo and that's the reason why they  are called them bright bodies  the embryo bodies are usually in italy  grown in suspension for about two weeks  and then they are plated for about one  week  in the first phases cells are  maintained in a culture in media that  it's not supplemented with cytokines or  small molecules  but is formulated so that the cell  survival  and the multiplication can can be  supported  so for example by stem max div base xf  the final analysis of the expression of  the various markers  can be done either by flow cytometry or  by  immunofluorescence  an alternative in the through test that  can be used to  quickly assess the pluripotency of a psc  line  is the trilineage differentiation assay  so this test is is based on the use of  cytokine containing a media  that allow the directed differentiation  into ectodermal mesodermal or endodermal  lineages  so if a line is a true psc line  it will be able to differentiate  here you may see a really schematic  protocol  for the standmax3 lineage  differentiation kit  so basically one psc clone is plated  into three separate walls  and each one of them will be fed with a  specific media from the kit  consistent with one of the three germ  layers  already at day seven as you can see the  cells are ready to be analyzed  also in this case we can  analyze the assay by classical  immunogenochemistry  or with flow cytometry  here as an example you can see the  assessment  that was performed with our affinity  recombinant  antibody panel the panel consists of  two marker combination for each germ  layer  and includes both surface and  intracellular markers  this is not only a fast readout of the  pluripotent potential of allow  of a line but it also allows us to  better understand  if our lines are biased towards the  differentiation  into one specific lineage  okay so now that we saw how to maintain  high quality pscs  we can actually move to the core aspect  of psc research so differentiation  so plc differentiation is achieved  by the sequential addition of patterning  factors  so cytokines small