ecules  for the differentiation  but it has a complex composition  that helps for cell survival and  proliferation  the the bayes media is then supplemented  with  different combination of cytokines and  small molecules  according of course to the  differentiation  pathway that we chose here you may see  the results of early differentiation  that was obtained by combining different  patterning factors  with standards max disbase itself as you  can see  this base medium can be used directly  without the need of cell adaptation  and supports direct differentiation both  in protocols with adherent culture  and in protocols that pass by a  eb stage  so how can we be sure to  use always the same amount of active  cytokine  in every differentiation round well  we always should assess the biological  activity  so that this can be maintained constant  among the experiment this is because  the cytokine specific biological  activity  can depend on the vendor and pro on the  production lot  this variation of course can lead to a  differentiation where  the culture is either over or under  saturated and of course the good  researchers should  assess every time that the cytokine  activity  is is the same or it should use  cytokines where the activity  values have already been measured so for  example  with the max premium grade cytokines  let's now come to the last point to  increase standardization that is cell  isolation  so as you know pst differentiation often  leads to a heterogeneous mixture of  cells  part of them are correctly  differentiated and  part of them have just a typical  phenotype  having a homogeneous population is of  course extremely  important so that we can have a reliable  results in our experiments and  of course measure the response just from  the correct  target cells enriching  the target cell population we can  control the percentage of pfc derived  cells  so that is consistent among various  experiments  why is this important well let's see  this example  on hepatocytes differentiation  so the cellular types descending from  the  endodermal lineage will pass through an  intermediate state that is known as  that is known as definitive endoderm  this initial patterning can be done in  vitro  by the addition of specific cytokine  such as for example active in  a then the sequential addition of other  cytokines and small molecules  will allow us for the terminal  differentiation  during the differentiation uh we  evaluated the  percentage of cells that were expressing  um definitive endotherm markers such as  fox a2 sox17 and  cxcr and what we saw  was that already at this  intermediate stage the cells actually  resemble  a heterogeneous population  as you can see from the flow datum  in fact just about a half of the cells  in population does not express  definitive endoderm  markers it's it's kind of clear that  this  will of course further reduce the  success  of our downstream differentiation into  for example hepatic cells  therefore we modified the original  protocol  by including an isolation step for the  definitive endoderm  by using the cd 184 micro bit kit  let's see how this has an impact on the  final population  so by comparing the expression of  hepatocytes markers  in cells obtain without  and with isolation step it's  clear that the letter shows a higher  expression of hypothesized marker  importantly of course the same isolation  strategy  can be used also for the other cell  types that  derive from the definitive endoderm  so we are now at the end of this webinar  and we saw how pluripotent stem cells  are  very sensitive cells and appropriate  cycle regions  and handling are of course key factors  in maintaining  your cells at their best moreover  we highlighted how psc characterization  should always be performed both after  reprogramming  but also as routine practice in  maintenance culture  finally we showed several different  strategies  that can be used to increase the  standardization during  psc differentiation  and with this i would like to thank you  all for your  attention  thank you viola for your informative  presentation  we will now start the q a portion of the  webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located  on the far left of your screen and we'll  answer as many questions as we have time  for  okay let's go ahead and get started our  first question  is in which case would you recommend  performing the trillionaire  differentiation assay  rather than the embryoid body's assay  yeah so well as i mentioned  of course both the tests are extremely  valuable and efficient to assess the  differentiation potential of a line  but that being said um it's clear that  that  lineage differentiation assay is is a  faster  alternative to the bright body assay  since it requires just seven days  compared to yeah about three weeks  and additionally also on a handling  point of view  the three lineage is way easier to  perform because you just have to  feed the cells with pre-made media so  i would personally uh suggest to  use the three lineage as say um as a  solution when we want to  do our routine assessment and we maybe  uh are assessing uh different  yeah many lines at the same time whereas  i would suggest to use the embryoid body  assay  just seldomly and just in the case we  are assessing just a  low number of lines so for example  when we are doing the first steps after  establishment of a new ipsc line so  after reprogramming  okay your next question what would you  suggest to do in case a psc line  fails a characterization assay  yeah yeah of course that unfortunately  this can happen  so uh clearly uh the first thing i  i would suggest to do is of course to  repeat the test  uh if we see that the line uh still  fails  the test and additionally for example is  starting to present  morphological alteration and yeah this  additional feature  then uh we can be pretty sure that the 